{#-
  The default `root-cause` kind's ticket body shape: every level-2 heading in order, each
  with the guidance it carries, the optional ones too. A verifying agent replaces every
  `<...>` placeholder and leaves out each optional section it has nothing for.

  Variables:
    rubric    the policy's rubric ({levels, areas, guidance, impact_lines, no_workaround,
              impact_parts, workaround_costs}); its table and prose are the `## Impact`
              guidance.
    fields    optional: the ticket's declared field values, a map name -> value (a value
              may also be a mapping carrying `description`, when the shape is rendered
              before any value is known). When non-empty, a `## Fields` section lists them.
-#}
{%- set parts = rubric.impact_parts -%}
{%- set lines = rubric.impact_lines -%}
{%- set costs = rubric.workaround_costs -%}
## Root cause

<plain prose, with no labelled parts, for a reader who knows what the project does but not
how it is built: the guarantee that is missing or broken, in the product's own terms — what
the system should do and does not, and what a user or developer sees when it fails. Name no
file, module, function, `path:line` or code identifier here; every one goes under
`## Suggested fix`'s `**Contributing locations.**`. A root cause is the **invariant or
guarantee that is missing or broken**, in words that do not depend on which file a report
happened to see. It is **one root cause when a single change to the rule removes every
instance, even if that change touches several places; two when they need independent fixes
that each stand alone.** The invariant is specific enough that one concrete suggested fix
removes it, so no catch-all ticket stands>

## Impact

<the {{ parts | length }} labelled parts below, each in one or two sentences, in this order;
then the {{ lines | length }} lines below, each exactly once, in this order, with nothing
between or after them. Both severities are judged by this rubric:

| | {{ rubric.areas.users }} | {{ rubric.areas.development }} | {{ rubric.areas.resources }} |
|---|---|---|---|
{% for level in rubric.levels -%}
| `{{ level.name }}` | {{ level.users | trim }} | {{ level.development | trim }} | {{ level.resources | trim }} |
{% endfor %}
{{ rubric.guidance | trim }}

The severity with the workaround follows from the {{ costs | length }} workaround costs you
state, rated on the rubric's scale, and is never above the severity. A workaround of exactly
`{{ rubric.no_workaround }}` leaves the two the same, and its `**{{ parts[-1] }}.**` part says
there is no acceptable workaround and why, in place of the bullets. Any other workaround
lowers a severity above `{{ (rubric.levels | last).name }}` at least one level. The last line,
the priority estimate and why, is written by `onefollowup ticket board-status` and never by
you>

**{{ parts[0] }}.** <each affected group by name: users of X, developers of Y, or spend only>
**{{ parts[1] }}.** <the concrete cost per occurrence, and how often it happens>
**{{ parts[2] }}.** <the impact itself, in plain words and with no label naming which area
decides: how many users lose what, how much a regularly used workflow slows, or the
$/month and quota share>
**{{ parts[3] }}.**
- {{ costs[0] }}: <effort, and who does it>
- {{ costs[1] }}: <what is lost or degraded, or none>
- {{ costs[2] }}: <the failure names it (free), or how long finding it takes and whether a
  person must step in>

- {{ lines[0] }}: <the severity with no workaround applied>
- {{ lines[1] }}: <the workaround in place and how it is applied, or {{ rubric.no_workaround }}>
- {{ lines[2] }}: <the severity that remains once the workaround is taken>
- Priority estimate: <written by `onefollowup ticket board-status`: the estimate and why>

## Examples

<one or more examples of it>

## Suggested fix

<one paragraph: what changes overall, and why that removes the root cause. The one fix this
ticket recommends, concrete enough that whoever picks it up has nothing left to choose; never
a list of options or alternatives to choose between>

**Contributing locations.**
- <repository name> `<path>`: <what this location does that breaks the guarantee>
- <one bullet per location that contributes, in whichever repository it is: every specific
  code reference this ticket carries belongs in this section>

### <unit> — `<repository>` (`<package or path>`)

<one subsection per unit that changes: a unit is a repository, or a part of a project, and
the parenthesis appears only when the unit is part of a repository. Say what changes in this
unit in a sentence or two, then show only the contracts that change — public interfaces, CLI
flags, schemas, output shapes and documented behaviour — each as a diff or the full shape. A
fix that changes behaviour with no interface change says so in one sentence. Leave out line
numbers, internal or private functions, refactoring steps, test names and implementation
walkthroughs: those are for whoever picks the ticket up to work out>

## Rejected fixes

<optional: leave this section out when no other fix was considered. Otherwise each fix that
was considered and not chosen, and why it was rejected>

## Owning batches

<every batch whose evidence this ticket carries>

{% if fields -%}
## Fields

{% for name, value in fields | items -%}
{% if value is mapping -%}
- {{ name }}: <{{ value.description | trim }}>
{% else -%}
- {{ name }}: {{ value }}
{% endif -%}
{% endfor %}
{% endif -%}
## Related tickets

<optional: leave this section out, heading and all, when it has no entries; never write
`none`. Each entry is one bullet and nothing more: every ticket this one depends on, marked
`dependency`, and each ticket that clearly addresses a similar root cause or suggests a
similar fix, at whatever status, marked `related`. An item at a proposal or deferred status is
only ever `related`. An entry is a link whose text is that ticket's title, never a bare URL,
its mark, and exactly one sentence saying why it is related to this ticket — for a
dependency, what this ticket needs from it — never a restatement of the other ticket. List
only a ticket clearly addressing a similar root cause, suggesting a similar fix, or supplying
a dependency this ticket needs. Mere shared area or a broader problem is insufficient.
Exclude a candidate meeting none of these three tests, and never mention a rejected
irrelevant candidate elsewhere, even in Evidence>

- [<that ticket's title>](<its URL>) — dependency: <one sentence: what this ticket needs from it>
- [<that ticket's title>](<its URL>) — related: <one sentence: why it is related to this ticket>

## Evidence

<the host the verification ran on, exactly as `hostname` printed it; then per draft or input:
its id, where it came from, the verified claim with `path:line` at the basis commit, and the
command that shows the transcript or source it was drafted from, where it names one>
